### Two Ways to Remember Who You Are

#### Session-Based Authentication

```
1. User logs in → server creates session record in DB/Redis
2. Server sends session ID in a cookie
3. Every request: browser sends cookie → server looks up session in store
4. Logout: delete session from store
```

```js
import session from 'express-session';
import RedisStore from 'connect-redis';

app.use(session({
  store: new RedisStore({ client: redisClient }),
  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
  resave: false,
  saveUninitialized: false,
  cookie: {
    httpOnly: true,   // JS can't read cookie
    secure: true,     // HTTPS only
    sameSite: 'lax',  // CSRF protection
    maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000 // 7 days
  }
}));

// Login
app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await authenticate(req.body);
  req.session.userId = user.id;
  res.json({ ok: true });
});

// Protected route
app.get('/profile', (req, res) => {
  if (!req.session.userId) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  // ...
});
```

#### JWT: Stateless Tokens

```
1. User logs in → server signs a JWT with user info
2. Server sends JWT to client (usually in response body)
3. Client stores JWT (memory or httpOnly cookie)
4. Every request: client sends JWT in Authorization header
5. Server verifies signature — no DB lookup needed
```

| | Sessions | JWT |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **State** | Server-stored | Client-stored (stateless) |
| **Revocation** | Easy (delete record) | Hard (need blocklist) |
| **Scalability** | Needs shared session store | Easy (any server can verify) |
| **Payload** | Only ID in cookie | Claims embedded in token |

