### Never Store Passwords in Plain Text

If your database is leaked, passwords must be unrecoverable. Use a slow, salted hash — **bcrypt**, **Argon2**, or **scrypt**.

```js
import bcrypt from 'bcrypt';

const SALT_ROUNDS = 12; // Higher = slower = more secure

// Registration: hash before storing
async function registerUser(email, password) {
  // Validate first
  if (password.length < 8) throw new Error('Password too short');

  const passwordHash = await bcrypt.hash(password, SALT_ROUNDS);
  return db.users.create({ email, passwordHash });
}

// Login: compare (bcrypt handles the salt automatically)
async function loginUser(email, password) {
  const user = await db.users.findByEmail(email);

  // Always compare even if user not found (prevents timing attacks)
  const isValid = user
    ? await bcrypt.compare(password, user.passwordHash)
    : await bcrypt.compare(password, '$2b$12$invalidhashtopreventtiming');

  if (!user || !isValid) throw new AppError('Invalid credentials', 401);
  return user;
}
```

#### Why Not MD5 / SHA-256?

These are fast cryptographic hashes. An attacker with a GPU can try **billions** of passwords per second against an MD5 hash. bcrypt is deliberately slow — ~100ms per check. A brute-force attack becomes impractical.

